{"code":0,"data":{"title":"Langfuse: Observability for LLM Applications","content":"# Langfuse: Observability for LLM Applications\n\nAfter an LLM app ships, developers quickly learn that a single user request often passes through prompt assembly, multiple model calls, RAG retrieval, tool use, and Agent decisions. When something goes wrong, you only see a bad final answer and struggle to tell which step drifted. Langfuse is an open-source observability platform built for that kind of black-box problem.\n\n## What is Langfuse[ #](#what-is-langfuse)\n\n**Langfuse** is an open-source LLM engineering / LLMOps observability platform for building, monitoring, debugging, and improving large-language-model apps and AI agents. It centers on **Tracing**: every model call, tool run, retrieval result, and state change inside a request is recorded as a structured Trace so teams can debug LLM pipelines the way they debug traditional microservices.\n\nCompared with **LangSmith**, which also targets LLM observability, Langfuse’s biggest difference is **open source and self-hosting**. LangSmith is a closed-source SaaS product from the LangChain team—capable, but usage-based past the free tier, and not fully deployable in a private environment. Langfuse is fully open under the **MIT license**, auditable in the open; you can use hosted **Langfuse Cloud** or self-host for free on your own infrastructure, with full control over data and cost.\n\nYou can use the project in two ways:\n\n* **Langfuse Cloud**: Official hosted SaaS, good for a quick start or small teams.\n* **Self-Hosted**: Private deploy with Docker Compose or Kubernetes, no Langfuse license fee, data stays on your infrastructure.\n\nWhether you call OpenAI / Anthropic / local models directly or go through LangChain, LlamaIndex, OpenTelemetry, and similar stacks, Langfuse can capture the important telemetry with relatively low instrumentation cost.\n\n## Core features[ #](#core-features)\n\nLangfuse’s capabilities follow three themes—observe, evaluate, iterate. The main modules are below.\n\n### Tracing[ #](#tracing)\n\nTracing is the foundation. Each user request maps to one **Trace**, broken along a timeline into **Spans** and **Generations**:\n\n* **Generation**: One LLM call—prompt input, model parameters, output, token usage, and latency.\n* **Span**: Any business step, such as RAG retrieval, tool calls, or knowledge-base queries.\n* **Event**: Notable events such as user feedback or errors.\n\nNested structure makes latency, cost, and intermediate state visible at each stage, so you can find hallucinations, timeouts, or retrieval failures faster.\n\n![tracing](/post/langfuseintro/ce00805a6ef8.png)\n\n### Metrics[ #](#metrics)\n\nThe platform aggregates Trace data into observability metrics:\n\n* Token usage and estimated cost\n* Latency distributions (P50 / P95 / P99)\n* Error rate and success rate\n* Model usage distribution\n\nYou can view them in the Dashboard or export via API into Prometheus / Grafana or your BI stack.\n\n![metrics](/post/langfuseintro/4f687f15edd8.png)\n\n![metrics\\_p](/post/langfuseintro/60d120180cea.png)\n\n### Evals[ #](#evals)\n\nLangfuse splits evaluation into two modes:\n\n* **Online**: After production traffic arrives, score automatically with rules, LLM-as-a-judge, or custom functions, and attach scores to concrete Traces.\n* **Offline**: Run batch evaluations on datasets for regression tests, A/B comparisons, and prompt-version comparisons.\n\nResults land as **Scores** on Traces or Generations so teams can quantify model quality and track trends.\n\n![evals\\_1](/post/langfuseintro/09e328649a1f.png)\n\n![evals\\_2](/post/langfuseintro/a458f23e4ff4.png)\n\n![evals\\_3](/post/langfuseintro/e0de38f9497f.png)\n\n### Prompt Management[ #](#prompt-management)\n\nLangfuse provides versioned prompt management:\n\n* Create, edit, and version prompt templates in the UI.\n* Call them in code by `prompt_name` and `version` so prompts are not scattered across the codebase.\n* Prompt changes can link directly to Traces, so you can see how version changes affect outputs.\n\n### Datasets[ #](#datasets)\n\nDatasets are for test sets and benchmarks:\n\n* Promote bad cases from production Traces into a dataset in one click.\n* Replay the same inputs to compare models or prompt versions.\n* Wire into CI for automatic regression before prompt or model changes ship.\n\n![datasets](/post/langfuseintro/3a920a4b821c.png)\n\n## Install and deploy[ #](#install-and-deploy)\n\nSelf-hosting Langfuse is Docker-first. Below is the core idea for a local trial and production; for exact image tags and the full variable list, prefer the [official self-host docs](https://langfuse.com/docs/deployment/self-host).\n\n### Local quick start[ #](#local-quick-start)\n\nThe official recommendation is Docker Compose. A minimal local stack usually includes:\n\n* `langfuse-web`: Web UI and API entrypoint.\n* `langfuse-worker`: Background jobs (batch writes, evaluation tasks, and so on).\n* `postgres`: Metadata and project data.\n* `clickhouse`: Trace and event storage.\n* `redis`: Job queue and cache.\n* `minio`: S3-compatible object storage (uploads, images, and similar).\n\nPrepare a `.env` file:\n\nbashCopy\n\n```bash\n# Database\nDATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@postgres:5432/langfuse\n\n# Distributed storage and queues\nCLICKHOUSE_URL=http://clickhouse:8123\nREDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379\n\n# S3-compatible object storage\nS3_ENDPOINT=http://minio:9000\nS3_BUCKET=langfuse\nS3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=minio\nS3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=miniosecret\n\n# Security and encryption (use strong random strings in production)\nNEXTAUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000\nNEXTAUTH_SECRET=your-nextauth-secret\nSALT=your-salt\nENCRYPTION_KEY=your-encryption-key\n```\n\nThen start in the same directory:\n\nbashCopy\n\n```bash\ndocker compose up -d\n```\n\nAfter the services are up, open `http://localhost:3000` to finish setup and create the first project.\n\n### Important environment variables[ #](#important-environment-variables)\n\n| Variable                                      | Role                                                                                           |\n| --------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `DATABASE_URL`                                | PostgreSQL connection string for users, projects, prompts, and other metadata.                 |\n| `CLICKHOUSE_URL`                              | ClickHouse connection string for large-scale Traces and events.                                |\n| `REDIS_URL`                                   | Redis connection string for queues and cache.                                                  |\n| `S3_*`                                        | Object-storage config; Trace attachments and dataset files depend on it.                       |\n| `NEXTAUTH_SECRET` / `SALT` / `ENCRYPTION_KEY` | Secrets for sessions, password hashing, and encryption—use strong random values in production. |\n| `NEXTAUTH_URL`                                | Auth callback URL; must match the public access URL.                                           |\n\n### Production notes[ #](#production-notes)\n\nWhen moving from a local trial to production, pay attention to:\n\n* **External DB and object storage**: Use managed PostgreSQL, Redis, and S3; avoid keeping state only inside containers.\n\n* **Persistence and backups**: ClickHouse and PostgreSQL need regular backups—Trace volume grows quickly.\n\n* **High availability**: Scale `langfuse-web` and `langfuse-worker` horizontally; HA for DB and object storage is separate.\n\n* **Security**:\n\n  * Manage encryption secrets (`NEXTAUTH_SECRET`, `SALT`, `ENCRYPTION_KEY`) via a secrets store; do not commit them to Git.\n  * Put HTTPS and appropriate access control in front of the web service.\n\n* **Capacity planning**: Trace volume scales with traffic; size ClickHouse and object storage against your retention policy.\n\nIf the team is not ready to operate Kubernetes, Langfuse Cloud is the lower-ops option: same product surface, infrastructure hosted by the vendor.\n\n## Basic usage[ #](#basic-usage)\n\nLangfuse ships Python and TypeScript/JavaScript SDKs, and also integrates through OpenTelemetry, LangChain, LlamaIndex, and similar frameworks. The examples below use the Python SDK for a minimal working path.\n\n### Get an API key[ #](#get-an-api-key)\n\nIn the Langfuse web UI, open project settings and create a **Public Key / Secret Key** pair. For a local deploy the host is `http://localhost:3000`; for Cloud it is `https://cloud.langfuse.com`.\n\n### Install the SDK[ #](#install-the-sdk)\n\nbashCopy\n\n```bash\npip install langfuse\n```\n\n### Option 1: Call the SDK directly[ #](#option-1-call-the-sdk-directly)\n\nUse this when you want precise control over Trace structure:\n\npythonCopy\n\n```python\nimport os\nfrom langfuse import Langfuse\n\nlangfuse = Langfuse(\n    public_key=os.getenv(\"LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY\"),\n    secret_key=os.getenv(\"LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY\"),\n    host=os.getenv(\"LANGFUSE_HOST\", \"http://localhost:3000\"),\n)\n\n# Create a Trace\ntrace = langfuse.trace(\n    name=\"qa-session\",\n    user_id=\"user-123\",\n    metadata={\"source\": \"web\"},\n)\n\n# Record an LLM call (Generation)\ntrace.generation(\n    name=\"openai-answer\",\n    model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n    input=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What is Langfuse?\"}],\n    output={\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"Langfuse is an open-source LLM observability platform.\"},\n    usage={\"input\": 12, \"output\": 18, \"unit\": \"TOKENS\"},\n)\n\n# Record a business step (Span)\nwith trace.span(name=\"retrieve-docs\") as span:\n    docs = [\"doc-1\", \"doc-2\"]\n    span.update(output={\"retrieved\": docs})\n\n# Flush events before the process exits\nlangfuse.flush()\n```\n\n### Option 2: Auto-trace with a decorator[ #](#option-2-auto-trace-with-a-decorator)\n\nIf you already call OpenAI directly, `@observe()` can instrument with almost no changes:\n\npythonCopy\n\n```python\nimport os\nfrom langfuse.decorators import observe\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(api_key=os.getenv(\"OPENAI_API_KEY\"))\n\n@observe()\ndef answer(question: str) -> str:\n    response = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}],\n    )\n    return response.choices[0].message.content\n\nanswer(\"What is Langfuse?\")\n```\n\nThe decorator creates a Trace for `answer`, turns inner LLM calls into Generations, and records output, latency, and token usage.\n\n### View in the Dashboard[ #](#view-in-the-dashboard)\n\nAfter running the code, open the Langfuse web UI for that project. You should see:\n\n* **Traces**: Requests on a timeline; open one for the full call chain.\n* **Generations**: All LLM calls, filterable by model, latency, and cost.\n* **Scores**: Manual or automatic scores on Traces to track quality trends.\n* **Dashboards**: Aggregated token use, latency, error rate, and more.\n\nFrom a Trace you can drill into a Generation and inspect the prompt, model parameters, and raw output to find the root cause faster.\n\n## Summary[ #](#summary)\n\n### Good fit for[ #](#good-fit-for)\n\nLangfuse works especially well for:\n\n* **RAG apps**: You need to connect retrieval, re-ranking, context assembly, and final generation.\n* **Agent systems**: Agents call models, tools, and external APIs many times; Traces show each decision and state change.\n* **Multi-model strategies**: When you call several models or fall back between them, you need latency, cost, and quality side by side.\n* **Cost-sensitive products**: Track tokens per user or session and spot abnormal or expensive traffic.\n* **Teams that iterate continuously**: Tie prompt versions, eval scores, and production feedback into one loop.\n\n### How it differs from similar tools[ #](#how-it-differs-from-similar-tools)\n\nThere are many LLM observability tools—LangSmith, Phoenix (Arize), Weights & Biases, and others. Langfuse stands out mainly because:\n\n* **Open source and self-hostable**: MIT license, Docker and Kubernetes self-hosting, full data ownership.\n* **Tracing-first**: Clear Trace structure with Generation / Span / Event layers for drill-down debugging.\n* **One platform**: Observability (Tracing/Metrics), evaluation (Evals), prompt management, and datasets in one place, less tool sprawl.\n* **Framework-agnostic**: Official SDKs plus OpenTelemetry, LangChain, LlamaIndex, Vercel AI SDK, and more.\n\n### Getting started[ #](#getting-started)\n\nFor teams new to Langfuse, a practical order is:\n\n1. **Validate on Cloud first**: Sign up for Langfuse Cloud, spend \\~30 minutes wiring a real endpoint, and confirm Traces look right.\n2. **Cover critical paths**: Add the SDK or decorator on core production calls so you can see what matters first.\n3. **Add metadata**: Put `user_id`, `session_id`, and `metadata` on Traces for later aggregation by user or session.\n4. **Introduce evals**: Start with simple rule scores or LLM-as-a-judge and build a quality baseline.\n5. **Version prompts**: Move critical prompts into Langfuse Prompt Management and link them to Traces.\n6. **Self-host when needed**: Move to Self-Hosted when compliance, cost, or customization requirements are clear.\n\nLangfuse is not a “install it and problems disappear” product. It gives you a structured observability vocabulary. The value shows up when the team folds Trace, Eval, and Prompt management into everyday development and iteration.\n","locale":"en","slug":"langfuseintro"}}